WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.801
A fictional statement shows a payment of about
88 dollars and 71 cents, but the balance fell
by less than that amount.

00:00:07.031 --> 00:00:08.161
Where did the rest go?

00:00:08.391 --> 00:00:15.634
In an amortizing loan, each scheduled payment
first covers interest accrued for that period;
the remaining amount reduces principal.

00:00:15.864 --> 00:00:21.396
Because the principal balance changes, the
next period’s interest is calculated on a
different amount.

00:00:21.626 --> 00:00:28.351
We will follow the classroom case’s simplified
one-thousand-dollar example using a stated
Canadian conversion convention.

00:00:28.431 --> 00:00:36.548
The model assumes a one-thousand-dollar
principal, a nominal annual rate of twelve
percent compounded semi-annually, and twelve
monthly payments.

00:00:36.778 --> 00:00:42.013
It excludes fees, missed or extra payments,
taxes, and changes to the contract.

00:00:42.243 --> 00:00:53.954
Under the Canadian convention used in this
example, convert the stated nominal rate to an
equivalent monthly rate: one plus point-twelve
divided by two, raised to the power of
two-twelfths, minus one.

00:00:54.184 --> 00:01:02.520
That is approximately 0.00975879, or 0.975879
percent per month.

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:06.848
Product documents can use different terms;
verify a real contract.

00:01:06.928 --> 00:01:15.580
For equal payments, the payment formula is
principal times the periodic rate divided by
one minus one plus the rate raised to negative
the number of payments.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:20.272
Substitute one thousand, the equivalent
monthly rate, and twelve periods.

00:01:20.502 --> 00:01:28.863
The calculated payment is about 88 dollars and
71 cents when displayed to cents; we keep the
unrounded amount internally for the
demonstration.

00:01:29.093 --> 00:01:35.695
Dividing twelve percent by twelve would use a
different convention and would not match the
stated semi-annual compounding assumption.

00:01:35.775 --> 00:01:42.170
In month one, interest is one thousand dollars
times the monthly rate, about 9 dollars and 76
cents.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:46.990
Subtract that from the unrounded payment of
about 88 dollars and 71 cents.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:55.205
Approximately 78 dollars and 95 cents reduces
principal, leaving a balance near 921 dollars
and 5 cents.

00:01:55.435 --> 00:02:00.639
For month two, calculate interest from the new
unrounded balance, not the original thousand.

00:02:00.869 --> 00:02:03.259
It is about 8 dollars and 99 cents.

00:02:03.489 --> 00:02:09.122
The remainder of the payment, about 79 dollars
and 73 cents, reduces principal.

00:02:09.202 --> 00:02:17.385
After month one, the displayed payment is
about $88.71 and the balance about $921.05.

00:02:17.615 --> 00:02:19.064
Estimate month-two interest.

00:02:19.294 --> 00:02:20.410
Pause and calculate.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:32.035
Using full precision, month-two interest is
about 8 dollars and 99 cents.

00:02:32.265 --> 00:02:41.999
Subtract that from the calculated payment and
roughly 79 dollars and 73 cents reduces
principal, leaving about 841 dollars and 32
cents.

00:02:42.229 --> 00:02:49.696
The common mistake is to calculate month-two
interest on the original thousand-dollar
balance or to say the entire payment reduces
principal.

00:02:49.926 --> 00:02:57.114
The model’s total interest over twelve exact
payments is about 64 dollars and 56 cents
before cent-rounding adjustments.

00:02:57.344 --> 00:03:02.452
These numbers depend on the stated convention
and assumptions; they are not a current rate
or an offer.

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:08.706
If comparing this schedule with another loan,
keep principal, payment frequency, and
assumptions visible.

00:03:08.936 --> 00:03:13.206
A smaller monthly payment may simply extend
the term and increase total interest.

00:03:13.436 --> 00:03:20.086
Add fees only when the offer states them, and
do not compare a weekly payment with a monthly
payment by looking at the number alone.

00:03:20.316 --> 00:03:26.646
The useful question is what the borrower pays
in total, when payments are due, and whether
the schedule remains affordable.

00:03:26.876 --> 00:03:34.234
Any real mortgage or loan uses its own
contract and calculation terms; this classroom
model is for understanding the balance
movement.

